sec.p:
The biophysical model is described as a 2-dimensional dynamical system with voltage variable v and adaptation variable a. The voltage variable (Equation 1) represents the neuron’s average membrane potential, which is related to membrane capacitance C and various trans-membrane ionic currents i. The adaptation variable (Equation 2) represents the neuron’s excitability, which the neuron depletes when active and recovers when quiet, and it evolves according to voltage-dependent functions for the steady-state adaptation a∞(v) and time constant τa(v).
(1)
C⋅dvdt=−ileak(v) Leak current −iexc(v) Excitatory current −iinh(v) Inhibitory current −iNaP(v,a) Persistent sodium current \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} C \cdot \frac{d v}{d t}=-\underset{\text { Leak current }}{i_{\text {leak }}(v)}-\underset{\text { Excitatory current }}{i_{\text {exc }}(v)}-\underset{\text { Inhibitory current }}{i_{\text {inh }}(v)}-\underset{\text { Persistent sodium current }}{i_{\text {NaP }}(v, a)} \tag{1} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(2)
τa(v)⋅dadt=a∞(v)−a \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \tau_{a}(v) \cdot \frac{d a}{d t}=a_{\infty}(v)-a \tag{2} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}

Such dynamics can be constructed through hybrid continuous-discrete rules. The adaptation variable (Equation 3) depletes towards 0 in the active state and recovers towards 1 in the quiet state, with adaptation time constant Ta′\begin{document}$T_{a}^{\prime}$\end{document}, scaling factor Ka(x), and bounds aactive(x) and aquiet(x), which are dependent on net input x. The adaptation evolves with an exponential trajectory, which approximates the slow variable trajectory in an averaged burst spiking model (Izhikevich, 2007):
(3)
Ta′⋅Ka(x)⋅dadt={0−aif active, inside bound:v≥0anda>aactive(x)1−aif quiet, inside bound:v<0anda<aquiet(x)0otherwise \begin{document}$$\displaystyle T_{a}^{\prime} \cdot K_{a}(x) \cdot \frac{d a}{d t}=\left\{\begin{array}{cll} 0-a & \text { if active, inside bound: } & v \geq 0 \text { and } a>a_{\text {active }}(x) \tag{3}\\ 1-a & \text { if quiet, inside bound: } & v<0 \text { and } a<a_{\text {quiet }}(x) \\ 0 & \text { otherwise } & \end{array}\right.$$\end{document}

The voltage variable (Equation 4) evolves based on a direction function d(v, a, x) ∈ {−1, 0, +1} and rate functions ractive(x) and rquiet(x), with instantaneous switches (of infinite voltage rate) handled by continuously constraining v to the range [−1, +1]:
(4)
dvdt=d(v,a,x)⋅{ractive(x)if active:v>0rquiet(x)if quiet:v<0∞if switch:v=0 \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \frac{d v}{d t}=d(v, a, x) \cdot\left\{\begin{array}{clc} r_{\text {active }}(x) & \text { if active: } & v>0 \tag{4}\\ r_{\text {quiet }}(x) & \text { if quiet: } & v<0 \\ \infty & \text { if switch: } & v=0 \end{array}\right.$$\end{document}

The voltage variable v ∈ ℝ represents the neuron’s average membrane potential governed by a differential equation relating membrane capacitance C and various trans-membrane ionic currents i, each with its own maximal conductance G and reversal potential V :
(6)
C⋅dvdt=−ileak(v) Leak current −iexc(v) Excitatory current −iinh(v) Inhibitory current −iPersistent sodium currentiNaP(v,a) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} C \cdot \frac{d v}{d t}=-\underset{\text { Leak current }}{i_{\text {leak }}(v)}-\underset{\text { Excitatory current }}{i_{\text {exc }}(v)}-\underset{\text { Inhibitory current }}{i_{\text {inh }}(v)}-i_{\text {Persistent sodium current }}^{i_{\text {NaP }}(v, a)} \tag{6} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}

The leak current maintains the resting membrane potential:
(7)
ileak(v)=Gleak⋅(v−Vleak) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle i_{\text {leak }}(v)=G_{\text {leak }} \cdot\left(v-V_{\text {leak }}\right)$$\end{document}

The excitatory and inhibitory synaptic currents depolarize and hyperpolarize, respectively, the membrane potential in response to constant drives D and synaptic input, summed over presynaptic neurons n with synaptic weights wn ≥ 0 and presynaptic firing activities y(vn) ≥ 0:
(8)
iexc(v)=Gexc⋅(v−Vexc)⋅[Dexc+∑nwnexcy(vn)] \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} i_{\text {exc }}(v)=G_{\text {exc }} \cdot\left(v-V_{\text {exc }}\right) \cdot\left[D_{\text {exc }}+\sum_{n} w_{n}^{\text {exc }} y\left(v_{n}\right)\right] \tag{8} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(9)
iinh(v)=Ginh⋅(v−Vinh)⋅[Dinh+∑nwninhy(vn)] \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} i_{\mathrm{inh}}(v)=G_{\mathrm{inh}} \cdot\left(v-V_{\mathrm{inh}}\right) \cdot\left[D_{\mathrm{inh}}+\sum_{n} w_{n}^{\mathrm{inh}} y\left(v_{n}\right)\right] \tag{9} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}

The persistent sodium (NaP) current produces bursting through its slow dynamics:
(10)
iNaP(v,a)=GNaP⋅(v−VNaP)⋅m∞(v)⋅a Activation Inactivation \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} i_{\mathrm{NaP}}(v, a)=G_{\mathrm{NaP}} \cdot\left(v-V_{\mathrm{NaP}}\right) \cdot m_{\infty}(v) \cdot \quad \underset{\text { Activation }}{a} \quad \text { Inactivation } \tag{10} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
where activation is modeled as instant with the steady-state activation m∞(v):
(11)
m∞(v)=(1+exp⁡(−v−VmKm))−1 \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} m_{\infty}(v)=\left(1+\exp \left(-\frac{v-V_{m}}{K_{m}}\right)\right)^{-1} \tag{11} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
and inactivation is modeled as slow with the adaptation variable a ∈ [0, 1] governed by a differential equation relating the steady-state inactivation a∞(v) and time constant τa(v):
(12)
τa(v)⋅dadt=a∞(v)−a \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \tau_{a}(v) \cdot \frac{d a}{d t}=a_{\infty}(v)-a \tag{12} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(13)
a∞(v)=(1+exp⁡(−v−VaKa))−1 \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} a_{\infty}(v)=\left(1+\exp \left(-\frac{v-V_{a}}{K_{a}}\right)\right)^{-1} \tag{13} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(14)
τa(v)=Ta+(Ta′−Ta)⋅(cosh⁡(v−VτaKτa))−1 \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \tau_{a}(v)=T_{a}+\left(T_{a}^{\prime}-T_{a}\right) \cdot\left(\cosh \left(\frac{v-V_{\tau_{a}}}{K_{\tau_{a}}}\right)\right)^{-1} \tag{14} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
and various parameters (V, K, T) control the shape of sigmoid and bell curves.

The firing activity of the neuron is a non-negative function of the voltage variable v:
(15)
y(v)=clip(v−VthresholdVmax−Vthreshold,0,1) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} y(v)=\operatorname{clip}\left(\frac{v-V_{\text {threshold }}}{V_{\max }-V_{\text {threshold }}}, 0,1\right) \tag{15} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}

For simulation, the differential equations for voltage (Equation 6) and adaptation (Equation 12) were discretized and integrated using the Forward-Euler method due to its simplicity with inseparable nonlinear terms:
(16)
vt+Δt=vt+ΔtC⋅[−ileak(vt)−iexc(vt)−iinh(vt)−iNaP(vt,at)] \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} v_{t+\Delta t}=v_{t}+\frac{\Delta t}{C} \cdot\left[-i_{\mathrm{leak}}\left(v_{t}\right)-i_{\mathrm{exc}}\left(v_{t}\right)-i_{\mathrm{inh}}\left(v_{t}\right)-i_{\mathrm{NaP}}\left(v_{t}, a_{t}\right)\right] \tag{16} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(17)
at+Δt=at+Δtτa(vt)⋅[a∞(vt)−at] \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} a_{t+\Delta t}=a_{t}+\frac{\Delta t}{\tau_{a}\left(v_{t}\right)} \cdot\left[a_{\infty}\left(v_{t}\right)-a_{t}\right] \tag{17} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}

The adaptation variable a ∈ (0, 1) depletes toward 0 (“empty”) in the active state and recovers toward 1 (“full”) in the quiet state, following an exponential trajectory that approaches, but never reaches, those asymptotic values:
(18)
Ta′⋅dadt={0−aif active:v=+11−aif quiet:v=−1Ta′=Ta4 \begin{document}$$\displaystyle T_{a}^{\prime} \cdot \frac{d a}{d t}=\left\{\begin{array}{lll} 0-a & \text { if active: } & v=+1 \tag{18}\\ 1-a & \text { if quiet: } & v=-1 \end{array} \quad T_{a}^{\prime}=\frac{T_{a}}{4}\right.$$\end{document}

The time spent on each leg is analytically derived by integrating the time-per-unit-adaptation between the (unknown) bounds. The time-per-unit-adaptation is the inverse of the adaptation-per-unit-time, which is the adaptation rate in Equation 18:
(19)
Tactive=∫aquietaactivedtdada=∫aquietaactiveTa′0−ada=Ta′⋅[ln⁡(aquiet)−ln⁡(aactive)]Tquiet=∫aactiveaquietdtdada=∫aactiveaquietTa′1−ada=Ta′⋅[ln⁡(1−aactive)−ln⁡(1−aquiet)] \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{align*} & T_{\text {active }}=\int_{a_{\text {quiet }}}^{a_{\text {active }}} \frac{d t}{d a} d a=\int_{a_{\text {quiet }}}^{a_{\text {active }}} \frac{T_{a}^{\prime}}{0-a} d a=T_{a}^{\prime} \cdot\left[\ln \left(a_{\text {quiet }}\right)-\ln \left(a_{\text {active }}\right)\right] \tag{19}\\ & T_{\text {quiet }}=\int_{a_{\text {active }}}^{a_{\text {quiet }}} \frac{d t}{d a} d a=\int_{a_{\text {active }}}^{a_{\text {quiet }}} \frac{T_{a}^{\prime}}{1-a} d a=T_{a}^{\prime} \cdot\left[\ln \left(1-a_{\text {active }}\right)-\ln \left(1-a_{\text {quiet }}\right)\right] \end{align*}$$\end{document}

This yields a system of 2 equations with 2 unknowns, which can easily be solved for the bounds by applying logarithmic properties, exponentiation, and substitution:
(20)
aactive=1−exp⁡(Tquiet′)1−exp⁡(Tactive′+Tquiet′)aquiet=aactive⋅exp⁡(Tactive′)Tactive′=TactiveTa′Tquiet′=TquietTa′ \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{align*} a_{\text {active }}=\frac{1-\exp \left(T_{\text {quiet }}^{\prime}\right)}{1-\exp \left(T_{\text {active }}^{\prime}+T_{\text {quiet }}^{\prime}\right)} & a_{\text {quiet }}=a_{\text {active }} \cdot \exp \left(T_{\text {active }}^{\prime}\right) \tag{20}\\ T_{\text {active }}^{\prime}=\frac{T_{\text {active }}}{T_{a}^{\prime}} & T_{\text {quiet }}^{\prime}=\frac{T_{\text {quiet }}}{T_{a}^{\prime}} \end{align*}$$\end{document}

The simplified model can modulate its properties in response to net input x, which includes bias input B and synaptic input, summed over presynaptic neurons n with synaptic weights wn ∈ ℝ and presynaptic firing activities yn ≥ 0:
(21)
x=B+∑nwnyn \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} x=B+\sum_{n} w_{n} y_{n} \tag{21} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}

The active and quiet bounds are also modified to be input-dependent by calculating the bounds from Equation 20 for no input (x = 0) and the strongest inputs (x = ±1):
(23)
Tactive+1=Tactive⋅Kactive+1Ta′⋅Ka,active+1Tquiet+1=Tquiet⋅Kquiet+1Ta′⋅Ka,quiet+1Tactive0=TactiveTa′Tquiet0=TquietTa′Tactive−1=Tactive⋅Kactive−1Ta′⋅Ka,active−1Tquiet−1=Tquiet⋅Kquiet−1Ta′⋅Ka,quiet−1aactivex=1−exp⁡(Tquietx)1−exp⁡(Tactivex+Tquietx)aquietx=aactivex⋅exp⁡(Tactivex) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{array}{ll} T_{\text {active }}^{+1}=\frac{T_{\text {active }} \cdot K_{\text {active }}^{+1}}{T_{a}^{\prime} \cdot K_{a, \text { active }}^{+1}} & T_{\text {quiet }}^{+1}=\frac{T_{\text {quiet }} \cdot K_{\text {quiet }}^{+1}}{T_{a}^{\prime} \cdot K_{a, \text { quiet }}^{+1}} \\ T_{\text {active }}^{0}=\frac{T_{\text {active }}}{T_{a}^{\prime}} & T_{\text {quiet }}^{0}=\frac{T_{\text {quiet }}}{T_{a}^{\prime}} \\ T_{\text {active }}^{-1}=\frac{T_{\text {active }} \cdot K_{\text {active }}^{-1}}{T_{a}^{\prime} \cdot K_{a, \text { active }}^{-1}} & T_{\text {quiet }}^{-1}=\frac{T_{\text {quiet }} \cdot K_{\text {quiet }}^{-1}}{T_{a}^{\prime} \cdot K_{a, \text { quiet }}^{-1}} \tag{23}\\ a_{\text {active }}^{x}=\frac{1-\exp \left(T_{\text {quiet }}^{x}\right)}{1-\exp \left(T_{\text {active }}^{x}+T_{\text {quiet }}^{x}\right)} & a_{\text {quiet }}^{x}=a_{\text {active }}^{x} \cdot \exp \left(T_{\text {active }}^{x}\right) \end{array}$$\end{document}
and then linearly interpolating between the bounds for intermediate inputs:
(24)
aactive(x)=interpolate(x;aactive−1,aactive0,aactive+1)aquiet(x)=interpolate(x;aquiet−1,aquiet0,aquiet+1) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{gather*} a_{\text {active }}(x)=\operatorname{interpolate}\left(x ; a_{\text {active }}^{-1}, a_{\text {active }}^{0}, a_{\text {active }}^{+1}\right) \tag{24}\\ a_{\text {quiet }}(x)=\operatorname{interpolate}\left(x ; a_{\text {quiet }}^{-1}, a_{\text {quiet }}^{0}, a_{\text {quiet }}^{+1}\right) \end{gather*}$$\end{document}

The simplified model can switch between states instantaneously or with some delay. Such delay can be useful for establishing phase relations between neurons (Figure 6) or enabling a period of competition between neurons during which state switches can be rejected (Figure 7). Specifically, delay is specified as a fixed fraction Dactive, Dquiet ∈ [0, 1] of the input-dependent state durations:
(25)
Tactive(x)=Ta′⋅Ka(x)⋅[ln⁡(aquiet(x))−ln⁡(aactive(x))]Tquiet(x)=Ta′⋅Ka(x)⋅[ln⁡(1−aactive(x))−ln⁡(1−aquiet(x))] \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{gather*} T_{\text {active }}(x)=T_{a}^{\prime} \cdot K_{a}(x) \cdot\left[\ln \left(a_{\text {quiet }}(x)\right)-\ln \left(a_{\text {active }}(x)\right)\right] \tag{25}\\ T_{\text {quiet }}(x)=T_{a}^{\prime} \cdot K_{a}(x) \cdot\left[\ln \left(1-a_{\text {active }}(x)\right)-\ln \left(1-a_{\text {quiet }}(x)\right)\right] \end{gather*}$$\end{document}

The adaptation variable differential equation from Equation 18 and Equation 22 is modified to speed up movement along horizontal segments and stop movement outside bounds:
(26)
Ta′⋅Ka(x)⋅dadt={0−a1−Dactiveif active, inside bound:v≥0anda>aactive(x)1−a1−Dquietif quiet, inside bound:v<0anda<aquiet(x)0otherwise \begin{document}$$\displaystyle T_{a}^{\prime} \cdot K_{a}(x) \cdot \frac{d a}{d t}=\left\{\begin{array}{cll} \frac{0-a}{1-D_{\text {active }}} & \text { if active, inside bound: } & v \geq 0 \text { and } a>a_{\text {active }}(x) \tag{26}\\ \frac{1-a}{1-D_{\text {quiet }}} & \text { if quiet, inside bound: } & v<0 \text { and } a<a_{\text {quiet }}(x) \\ 0 & \text { otherwise } & \end{array}\right.$$\end{document}

The voltage variable direction function is modified to reverse the direction of movement along vertical segments in response to net input:
(29)
d(v,a,x)={sign(+1−v)if active, inside bound or stable bound:v≥0and[(a>aactive(x))or(a=aactive(x)andx>Xactive)]sign(0−v)if active, otherwise:v≥0sign(−1−v)if quiet, inside bound or stable bound:v<0and[(a<aquiet(x))or(a=aquiet(x)andx<Xquiet)]sign(0−v)if quiet, otherwise:v<0 \begin{document}$$\displaystyle d(v, a, x)= \begin{cases}\operatorname{sign}(+1-v) & \text { if active, inside bound or stable bound: } \tag{29}\\ & v \geq 0 \text { and }\left[\left(a>a_{\text {active }}(x)\right) \text { or }\left(a=a_{\text {active }}(x) \text { and } x>X_{\text {active }}\right)\right] \\ \operatorname{sign}(0-v) & \text { if active, otherwise: } \\ & v \geq 0 \\ \operatorname{sign}(-1-v) & \text { if quiet, inside bound or stable bound: } \\ & v<0 \text { and }\left[\left(a<a_{\text {quiet }}(x)\right) \text { or }\left(a=a_{\text {quiet }}(x) \text { and } x<X_{\text {quiet }}\right)\right] \\ \operatorname{sign}(0-v) & \text { if quiet, otherwise: } \\ & v<0\end{cases}$$\end{document}

The adaptation variable dynamics is modified to include an instantaneous constraint that clips adaptation to bounds if inside bound margins ãactive(x), ãquiet(x):
(30)
a←{aactive(x)if active, inside bound margin:v≥0anda~active(x)≤a≤aactive(x)aquiet(x)if quiet, inside bound margin:v<0andaquiet(x)≤a≤a~quiet(x) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle a \leftarrow\left\{\begin{array}{ccl} a_{\text {active }}(x) & \text { if active, inside bound margin: } & v \geq 0 \text { and } \tilde{a}_{\text {active }}(x) \leq a \leq a_{\text {active }}(x) \tag{30}\\ a_{\text {quiet }}(x) & \text { if quiet, inside bound margin: } & v<0 \text { and } a_{\text {quiet }}(x) \leq a \leq \tilde{a}_{\text {quiet }}(x) \end{array}\right.$$\end{document}
where the bound margins are calculated from time margins δactive, δquiet similar to in Equation 19 and Equation 20 (derivation omitted):
(31)
a~active(x)=aactive(x)⋅exp⁡(−δactiveTa′⋅Ka(x))a~quiet(x)=1−(1−aquiet(x))⋅exp⁡(−δquietTa′⋅Ka(x)) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \tilde{a}_{\text {active }}(x)=a_{\text {active }}(x) \cdot \exp \left(-\frac{\delta_{\text {active }}}{T_{a}^{\prime} \cdot K_{a}(x)}\right) \quad \tilde{a}_{\text {quiet }}(x)=1-\left(1-a_{\text {quiet }}(x)\right) \cdot \exp \left(-\frac{\delta_{\text {quiet }}}{T_{a}^{\prime} \cdot K_{a}(x)}\right) \tag{31} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}

The simplified model can incorporate noise into the dynamics, which is useful for increasing variability and breaking symmetries. Specifically, it scales voltage and adaptation rates with non-negative multiplicative noise ε sampled at each timestep from a normal distribution with mean 1 and standard deviation σ:
(32)
ε=max(ε′,0),ε′∼N(1,σ2) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \varepsilon=\max \left(\varepsilon^{\prime}, 0\right), \quad \varepsilon^{\prime} \sim \mathcal{N}\left(1, \sigma^{2}\right) \tag{32} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}

A simple rectangular waveform can use constant values along both active and quiet legs:
(33)
yrectangular(v,a,x)={Yactiveif activev≥0Yquietif quiet:v<0 \begin{document}$$\displaystyle y_{\text {rectangular }}(v, a, x)=\left\{\begin{array}{lll} Y_{\text {active }} & \text { if active } & v \geq 0 \tag{33}\\ Y_{\text {quiet }} & \text { if quiet: } & v<0 \end{array}\right.$$\end{document}
or an adapting waveform can linearly interpolate between high and low values along the active leg:
(34)
yadapting(v,a,x)={linear(a;Yactivelow,Yactivehigh)if activev≥0Yquietif quiet:v<0 \begin{document}$$\displaystyle y_{\text {adapting }}(v, a, x)=\left\{\begin{array}{cl} \operatorname{linear}\left(a ; Y_{\text {active }}^{\text {low }}, Y_{\text {active }}^{\text {high }}\right) & \text { if active } \quad v \geq 0 \tag{34}\\ Y_{\text {quiet }} & \text { if quiet: } \quad v<0 \end{array}\right.$$\end{document}
where linear interpolation between endpoint values A, B uses z ∈ [0, 1]:
(35)
linear(z;A,B)=A⋅(1−z)+B⋅z \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \operatorname{linear}(z ; A, B)=A \cdot(1-z)+B \cdot z \tag{35} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}

A more controllable curved waveform can set exact values at the bounds and adjust the curve to be convex, concave, or approximately linear (by adjusting C):
(36)
ycurved(v,a,x)={logarithmic(z;Yactiveend,Yactivestart,C)if activeYquietif quiet:v<0z=normalize(a;aactive(x),aquiet(x)) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{gather*} y_{\text {curved }}(v, a, x)=\left\{\begin{array}{cl} \operatorname{logarithmic}\left(z ; Y_{\text {active }}^{\text {end }}, Y_{\text {active }}^{\text {start }}, C\right) & \text { if active } \\ Y_{\text {quiet }} & \text { if quiet: } \end{array} \quad \begin{array}{c} v<0 \\ z=\operatorname{normalize}\left(a ; a_{\text {active }}(x), a_{\text {quiet }}(x)\right) \end{array}\right. \tag{36} \end{gather*}$$\end{document}
where logarithmic interpolation between endpoint values A, B uses z ∈ [0, 1] and base C > 0:
(37)
logarithmic(z;A,B,C)={A+(B−A)⋅logC⁡(1+(C−1)⋅z)C≠1linear(z;A,B)C=1 \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \text { logarithmic }(z ; A, B, C)=\left\{\begin{array}{cc} A+(B-A) \cdot \log _{C}(1+(C-1) \cdot z) & C \neq 1 \tag{37}\\ \operatorname{linear}(z ; A, B) & C=1 \end{array}\right.$$\end{document}
and bounds normalization between bound locations A, B uses u ∈ ℝ:
(38)
normalize(u;A,B)=clip(u−AB−A,0,1) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \text { normalize }(u ; A, B)=\operatorname{clip}\left(\frac{u-A}{B-A}, 0,1\right) \tag{38} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}

First, the current adaptation is clipped to the new bounds if within time margins (Equation 30). Second, the new adaptation is calculated from the dynamics, using a newly sampled noise:
(39)
at+Δt={at1+u,u=Δt⋅εtTa′⋅Ka(xt)⋅(1−Dactive)if active, inside bound:at+u1+u,u=Δt⋅εtTa′⋅Ka(xt)⋅(1−Dquiet)if quiet, inside bound:atvt<0andat<aquiet(xt)otherwise \begin{document}$$\displaystyle a_{t+\Delta t}=\left\{\begin{array}{ccl} \frac{a_{t}}{1+u}, & u=\frac{\Delta t \cdot \varepsilon_{t}}{T_{a}^{\prime} \cdot K_{a}\left(x_{t}\right) \cdot\left(1-D_{\text {active }}\right)} & \text { if active, inside bound: } \tag{39}\\ \frac{a_{t}+u}{1+u}, & u=\frac{\Delta t \cdot \varepsilon_{t}}{T_{a}^{\prime} \cdot K_{a}\left(x_{t}\right) \cdot\left(1-D_{\text {quiet }}\right)} & \text { if quiet, inside bound: } \\ & a_{t} & v_{t}<0 \text { and } a_{t}<a_{\text {quiet }}\left(x_{t}\right) \\ & \text { otherwise } \end{array}\right.$$\end{document}

Fourth, the new voltage is calculated from the dynamics, using the new adaptation to move in the correct direction along the bounds:
(40)
vt+Δt=vt+Δt⋅d(vt,at+Δt,xt)⋅εt⋅{1Dactive⋅Tactive(xt)if active:vt>01Dquiet⋅Tquiet(xt)if quiet:vt<0 \begin{document}$$\displaystyle v_{t+\Delta t}=v_{t}+\Delta t \cdot d\left(v_{t}, a_{t+\Delta t}, x_{t}\right) \cdot \varepsilon_{t} \cdot\left\{\begin{array}{lll} \frac{1}{D_{\text {active }} \cdot T_{\text {active }}\left(x_{t}\right)} & \text { if active: } & v_{t}>0 \tag{40}\\ \frac{1}{D_{\text {quiet }} \cdot T_{\text {quiet }}\left(x_{t}\right)} & \text { if quiet: } & v_{t}<0 \end{array}\right.$$\end{document}

Single-unit experiments were run using a consistent set of parameters, and an additional parameter I was introduced to net input x to represent stimulus input. For the biophysical model, parameters were used from Kim et al. (2022), which hand-tuned them to match experimental observations of cat locomotion (Table 1). For the simplified model, parameters were hand-tuned to match the biophysical model in response to constant, pulse, and periodic stimuli (Table 2). The firing function used a customized ycurved to approximate the biophysical model’s input-dependent changes to nullcline-v (and therefore the shape of its activity waveform):
(41)
z=normalize(a;aactive+1,aquiet0)Yactivestart=linear(clip(x,0,1);0.85,0.9)Yactiveend=linear(clip(x,0,1);0.05,0.2)Yquiet=linear(normalize(x;0.8,1);0,0.1)C=20 \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{gather*} z=\operatorname{normalize}\left(a ; a_{\text {active }}^{+1}, a_{\text {quiet }}^{0}\right) \\ Y_{\text {active }}^{\text {start }}=\operatorname{linear}(\operatorname{clip}(x, 0,1) ; 0.85,0.9) \\ Y_{\text {active }}^{\text {end }}=\operatorname{linear}(\operatorname{clip}(x, 0,1) ; 0.05,0.2) \tag{41}\\ Y_{\text {quiet }}=\operatorname{linear}(\operatorname{normalize}(x ; 0.8,1) ; 0,0.1) \\ C=20 \end{gather*}$$\end{document}

Pulse stimuli of various strengths and phases were applied to assess phase shifts. Pulses were 100 ms in duration. At each strength, pulses were tested across the intrinsic cycle at pulse start times separated by 25 ms. Phase shift Δϕ was quantified by comparing the perturbed cycle duration T ′ to the intrinsic cycle duration T :
(42)
Δϕ=T′−TT \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \Delta \phi=\frac{T^{\prime}-T}{T} \tag{42} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}

Entrainment was quantified by phase coherence. For each response waveform, burst peaks were identified and assigned phases ϕ ∈ [0, 1) relative to the stimulus. These phases converge to a steady-state value under entrainment, and their circular mean characterizes convergence speed and robustness. The circular mean phase ϕ¯\begin{document}$\bar{\phi}$\end{document} was calculated across pulses and initial pulse phases:
(43)
ϕ¯=1Npulses⋅Ninits∑ϕe2πiϕ \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \bar{\phi}=\frac{1}{N_{\text {pulses }} \cdot N_{\text {inits }}} \sum_{\phi} e^{2 \pi i \phi} \tag{43} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}

Circuits use basic neurons in addition to bursting neurons. The simplified model of basic neurons used for experiments has standard linear dynamics that can modulate firing rate in response to net input x, which includes bias input B and synaptic input, summed over presynaptic neurons n with synaptic weights wn ∈ ℝ and presynaptic firing activities yn ≥ 0:
(44)
x=clip(B+∑nwnyn,−1,+1) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} x=\operatorname{clip}\left(B+\sum_{n} w_{n} y_{n},-1,+1\right) \tag{44} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(45)
Tv′⋅dvdt=x−vTv′=Tv4 \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} T_{v}^{\prime} \cdot \frac{d v}{d t}=x-v \quad T_{v}^{\prime}=\frac{T_{v}}{4} \tag{45} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}

The firing activity of the neuron is a non-negative function of the voltage variable v:
(46)
y(v)=clip(v,0,1) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} y(v)=\operatorname{clip}(v, 0,1) \tag{46} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}

For simulation, the differential equation for voltage was discretized and integrated using the Backward-Euler method due to its stability and tractability with linear terms:
(47)
vt+Δt=vt+xt⋅u1+u,u=ΔtTv′ \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} v_{t+\Delta t}=\frac{v_{t}+x_{t} \cdot u}{1+u}, \quad u=\frac{\Delta t}{T_{v}^{\prime}} \tag{47} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
